//! member: true
//!
//! std.io — standard output, standard error, and standard input as
//! functions: the facade over the language's `print` family and the s38
//! line-read builtin. Stability tier: core.
//!
//! **Why a facade at all, when `print` is ambient.** `print` is a
//! prelude intrinsic and stays the spelling ordinary code writes — this
//! module does not replace it and could not (§1: one concept, one name).
//! What it adds is the four operations as VALUES of the library rather
//! than magic: a stream chosen by the function you call rather than by a
//! name's prefix, the raw-versus-line difference spelled in the name, and
//! one home for the input side, whose row is the interesting part and
//! whose ambient spelling std may not reuse (see below). A program that
//! computes what to write, or writes to stderr from a helper, reads
//! better through these.
//!
//! **The format spec is the CALLER's, and it passes through untouched.**
//! Every function here takes an already-rendered `str`, so
//! `io.write_line("{n:>8}")` is the caller's f-string with the caller's
//! spec (spec §7.4, the s38 lowering) and this module adds nothing to it
//! — no second interpolation pass, no reformatting, nothing that could
//! disagree with what the literal says. The operations that BUILD a
//! rendered string out of data live in `std.fmt` (§11's split), and this
//! module is where the result goes.
//!
//! **Capability: `io` (I13).** The four writers and the line read reach the
//! host's standard streams, are tagged `io` in the toolchain's sandbox
//! table, and are categorically refused at comptime (D33): a `comptime
//! fn` cannot read a line, and — because a comptime program's output
//! would be neither reproducible nor visible — cannot write one either.
//!
//! **Writing cannot fail here, and that is the builtin's contract, not a
//! simplification.** `print`/`eprint` return nothing and raise nothing:
//! a closed stdout is not reportable at this pin. When the builtin tier
//! grows a fallible write, these signatures gain a row and the change is
//! a breaking one, which is why it is recorded here rather than
//! pre-empted with a row that would always miss (F-0046).
//!
//! **The input side is two functions and one row between them.**
//! `input_line` raises `eof` at the end of input — an outcome, not a
//! failure — plus `io` for a host failure and `utf8` for bytes that are not
//! text. `input_all` reads to the end and joins the lines with `"\n"`, so
//! `eof` is not in ITS row at all: the end of input is what makes it
//! return.
//!
//! `input_all` was a reviewed contract for four sprints and it is worth
//! recording why, because the reason was never "nobody wrote it". The loop
//! must stop on ONE tag and re-raise the others, and no handler could tell
//! tags apart: a bare identifier in a handler BINDS rather than matching, so
//! it catches everything (F-0043), and the shape that should have fixed it —
//! `else |e| match e { eof => …, io => … }` — matched its FIRST ARM for
//! every tag on the lane that executed, with no diagnostic (F-0052). A loop
//! written over that would have treated a read error as the end of input,
//! which is a silent truncation of someone's data, and §14 forbids shipping
//! an operation whose failure mode a module cannot distinguish. s70's match
//! tier and s71's payload-pattern ruling closed both, and the function is
//! four lines and one handler.
//!
//! What is still a reviewed contract is the byte-exact read:
//!
//! - `read_bytes(max: int) -> List[int] ! {io}` — standard input as it is.
//!   `input_all` joins LINES, and the line read strips terminators, so
//!   `"a\nb"` and `"a\nb\n"` are indistinguishable through this module —
//!   which is fine for text processing and wrong for anything byte-exact.
//!   No builtin returns bytes from standard input (F-0046, unmoved), so
//!   std ships nothing rather than a `read_bytes` that could only lie about
//!   the last byte.
//!
//! **Why the reader is `input_line` and not `read_line`.** `read_line` is
//! the name this module wanted, and it is a name std cannot have: it is an
//! AMBIENT PRELUDE name, and a module item that shares one is resolved
//! differently by the two implementations. Measured at these pins, from a
//! `pub fn read_line` whose body called `read_line()`: wolfc resolved the
//! inner call to the BUILTIN (the delegation worked), and lupin resolved
//! it to the module's own item — unbounded recursion, reported as
//! `unsupported — call depth exceeded 512 frames`. One source, a facade in
//! one machine and a stack overflow in the other, with no diagnostic on
//! either side (F-0047). So the reader takes a name of its own, the
//! writers keep theirs (nothing ambient is called `write`), and the filing
//! asks for what would let the good name come back: retire the ambient
//! host names — the prelude's own comment already promises to, "the moment
//! the real std surface replaces them" — and rule the shadowing question
//! explicitly for the names that stay.
//!
//! **Lanes (re-measured at the sc11 pin).** Both compiler rungs execute
//! everything here — the native rung lowers the io tier as of #40, so both
//! of this module's tests run natively where both were `unsupported` in
//! sc07. Standard input is still EMPTY on the checked lane (`conform-run`
//! has no stdin injection at this pin, F-0046 unmoved), so the `eof` path
//! is the one the rig witnesses and the hit path is proven upstream. The
//! writers' stdout is compared by hash; stderr is not in the record
//! protocol, so `write_err` and `write_err_line` are witnessed by their
//! exit status alone, which is stated rather than hidden. lupin gained
//! `eprint`/`eprint_raw` at 0.1.5, which is what moved
//! `tests/io/writers_and_streams.lu` to a lupin `run` row: all four
//! writers work there now and only the READS are refused (this machine has
//! no `read_line` and no injectable stdin, by design and with a sentence in
//! the refusal — unchanged at 0.1.8). The capability posture is the rig's to
//! record once rather than each test's to restate.

/// Write `text` to standard output exactly as given — no newline, no
/// reformatting.
///
/// Total: no rows, no traps (see the module header on the builtin's
/// infallible-write contract). Capability `io`; refused at comptime
/// (D33). Delegates to `print_raw`.
///
/// This is the function to build a line out of pieces with, and the one
/// to use when the bytes must be exact: a progress indicator, a prompt,
/// a value that must not gain a terminator it did not ask for.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// io.write("no newline here")
/// io.write("")
/// ```
pub fn write(text: str) {
    print_raw(text)
}

/// Write `text` to standard output followed by a newline.
///
/// Total: no rows, no traps. Capability `io`; refused at comptime (D33).
/// Delegates to `print`, whose terminator is exactly one `"\n"` on every
/// platform — no `"\r\n"` translation anywhere, which is what keeps a
/// stdout hash comparable across the tier-1 hosts.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// io.write_line("one line")
/// io.write_line("{2 + 2}")
/// ```
pub fn write_line(text: str) {
    print(text)
}

/// Write `text` to standard error exactly as given — no newline.
///
/// Total: no rows, no traps. Capability `io`; refused at comptime (D33).
/// Delegates to `eprint_raw`.
///
/// Standard error is the stream for what a program says ABOUT its work
/// rather than what its work produced: diagnostics, progress, warnings.
/// Keeping them off stdout is what lets a caller pipe the output of a
/// wolf program into another one — and it is why the rig can hash stdout
/// at all.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// io.write_err("partial diagnostic ")
/// io.write_err("continued\n")
/// ```
pub fn write_err(text: str) {
    eprint_raw(text)
}

/// Write `text` to standard error followed by a newline.
///
/// Total: no rows, no traps. Capability `io`; refused at comptime (D33).
/// Delegates to `eprint`.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// io.write_err_line("a diagnostic line")
/// ```
pub fn write_err_line(text: str) {
    eprint(text)
}

/// The next line of standard input, without its terminator.
///
/// Rows: `eof` when there is no more input — the END of the stream is an
/// outcome, so a read loop is `while` over this function's misses rather
/// than a length test; `io` for a host failure; `utf8` when the bytes are
/// not well-formed UTF-8 (nothing is returned in that case — this is not
/// a lossy decode). Never traps.
///
/// The terminator is consumed and dropped, and a `"\r\n"` ending yields
/// the line without either byte — so a file written on windows reads the
/// same as one written on linux, and a caller cannot tell whether the
/// last line of the input ended with a newline. That last point is a real
/// limitation of the line-oriented shape, not a bug: a caller who needs
/// the bytes as they are wants `read_all`, which is a contract at this
/// pin (F-0046).
///
/// Capability `io`; refused at comptime (D33). Delegates to the prelude's
/// `read_line`, whose name this function deliberately does not share —
/// see the module header.
///
/// A doc example cannot demonstrate the hit path: an extracted example
/// runs with no stdin, so the honest example is the miss, which is also
/// the one every reader gets wrong first.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// (io.input_line() else "no input") == "no input"
/// ```
pub fn input_line() -> str ! {eof, io, utf8} {
    read_line()?
}

/// Write `text` to standard output with no newline, then read one line
/// of standard input.
///
/// Rows: `input_line`'s exactly — `eof` when the input ends (the user
/// pressed the terminal's end-of-input, or the pipe closed), `io` for a
/// host failure, `utf8` for bytes that are not text. Never traps.
///
/// The prompt is written with `write` rather than `write_line`, because a
/// prompt belongs on the same line as the answer, and it is not flushed
/// separately: standard output is line-buffered on a terminal, so a
/// prompt with no newline may appear only when the line is read. That is
/// the host's behaviour and this function does not paper over it; a
/// program that needs a guaranteed flush before a blocking read wants
/// the flush operation, which no builtin offers yet (F-0046).
///
/// Capability `io`; refused at comptime (D33).
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// (io.prompt("name? ") else "anonymous") == "anonymous"
/// ```
pub fn prompt(text: str) -> str ! {eof, io, utf8} {
    print_raw(text)
    read_line()?
}

/// Every remaining line of standard input, joined with `"\n"`.
///
/// Rows: `io` for a host failure and `utf8` for bytes that are not text —
/// both RE-RAISED unchanged from the line read they came from. `eof` is not
/// in the row: the end of input is what makes this function RETURN, which
/// is the whole difference between reading everything and failing to read
/// something. Never traps.
///
/// **What it can and cannot tell you.** The result is the input's lines
/// with exactly one `"\n"` between them and NONE at the end, whatever the
/// input's own last byte was: the line read strips the terminator and
/// nothing gives it back, so `"a\nb"` and `"a\nb\n"` both arrive here as
/// `"a\nb"` (F-0046 asks for the byte-exact `read_all` that would fix it).
/// A caller who must know wants that builtin; a caller who wants lines is
/// not affected, and `std.str.lines` on this result gives them back.
/// Empty input is `""`, which is also what a single empty line looks like,
/// for the same reason.
///
/// **Why this function exists only now.** It was a reviewed contract for
/// four sprints because the loop it needs must stop on ONE tag and re-raise
/// the others, and no handler could tell tags apart: a bare binding catches
/// everything (F-0043) and a `match` inside a handler matched its first arm
/// for every tag on the lane that executed (F-0052). Both are closed at
/// this pin — the match tier at s70, the payload pattern at s71 — so the
/// handler below discriminates on all three lanes, and the alternative std
/// refused to ship is visible in what it would have done: treated a read
/// error as the end of input and truncated someone's data (§14).
///
/// Capability `io`; refused at comptime (D33). Delegates to the prelude's
/// `read_line`, once per line.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// (io.input_all() else "?") == ""
/// ```
pub fn input_all() -> str ! {io, utf8} {
    var out = ""
    var lines = 0
    var more = true
    while more {
        let line = read_line() else |e| match e {
            eof => {
                more = false
                ""
            },
            io => {
                return io
            },
            utf8 => {
                return utf8
            },
        }
        if more {
            if lines == 0 {
                out = line
            } else {
                out = "{out}\n{line}"
            }
            lines = lines + 1
        }
    }
    out
}
